# Supply Chain

This project follows a defense-in-depth supply-chain posture. Every rule
here is enforced — not aspirational.

## Container base images

Only `cgr.dev/chainguard/*` images are permitted. Forbidden: `python:3.x`,
`node:20`, `alpine`, `debian`, `ubuntu`, `bitnami/*`, `bullseye`, `slim`.

The dev variant is `:latest-dev` (has shell, package manager, `uv`/`pip`).
The minimal variant is `:latest` (no shell, no package manager). Use
multi-stage builds: install in `-dev`, copy artifacts to `:latest`.

The Chainguard Python `:latest-dev` image already includes `uv`, so no
separate uv install step is needed.

Production stage runs as the `nonroot` user (UID 65532). Do not switch
to root in the production stage. If `apk add` is needed during build,
use `USER root` in the dev stage only.

## Dependency lockfiles

- Python: `uv.lock` is committed and authoritative. Allowed installs:
  - `uv sync --frozen` (CI, Docker, fresh clone)
  - `uv add <pkg>` (interactive, with user approval)
  - `uv lock --upgrade-package <pkg>` (interactive, with user approval)
- JS: `pnpm-lock.yaml` is committed and authoritative. Allowed installs:
  - `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` (CI, Docker, fresh clone)
  - `pnpm add <pkg>` (interactive, with user approval)

`pip install`, `npm install`, `yarn install` are forbidden anywhere —
including throwaway scripts and Dockerfiles.

## Network install patterns

The `curl <url> | sh` pattern is forbidden in Dockerfiles, scripts, and
CI. If a tool requires it for installation, propose an alternative
(Chainguard image, Wolfi package, or pinned download with checksum).

GitHub Actions must be pinned. `uses: actions/checkout@v5` is acceptable
(major version with security commitments). `@main` or `@master` is not.
Prefer pinning to a SHA for third-party actions.

## Pre-commit checks (blocking)

Configured in `lefthook.yml`:
- `gitleaks` scans staged content for secrets — blocks on detection
- `ruff check` and `ruff format --check` on Python diff
- `biome check` on JS/TS diff
- `hadolint` on Dockerfile diff
- `shellcheck` on `.sh` diff

A commit cannot be made if any of the above fail. Do not advise the user
to bypass with `--no-verify`. If a check is failing wrongly, fix the
check or the file — don't bypass.

## CI checks

- `cosign verify` against the Chainguard images used in Dockerfiles
- `pip-audit` against `uv.lock`
- `pnpm audit --prod`
- `osv-scanner` as a backstop (catches both ecosystems)
- `syft` produces an SBOM artifact attached to every CI run

## Renovate

`renovate.json` configures Renovate to:
- Auto-merge dev-tooling minor/patch updates after CI passes
- Open PRs (in this repo only — overrides the no-PR rule for bot-driven
  dep updates) for app-dep major versions
- Pin GitHub Actions to SHAs

## Forbidden practices

- Editing `uv.lock` or `pnpm-lock.yaml` by hand
- Committing `.env` or any file matching `*.pem`, `*.key`, `id_rsa*`
- Hardcoding API keys or tokens in source, even as "TODO replace later"
- Running `chmod -R` in Dockerfiles
- `ADD <url>` in Dockerfiles (use multi-stage with explicit verification)
